{
  "topic": "childcare",
  "title": "Childcare prices by county",
  "asOf": "2026-10",
  "dataYear": 2022,
  "methodology": "County prices come from the U.S. Department of Labor Women's Bureau National Database of Childcare Prices (NDCP), 2008-2022 file, which DOL built from state childcare market-rate surveys. We use the NDCP's aggregated weekly full-time median price variables: center-based care MCINFANT (0-23 months), MCTODDLER (24-35 months), MCPRESCHOOL (36-54 months) and MCSA (school age), and family child care (\"home\") MFCCINFANT, MFCCTODDLER, MFCCPRESCHOOL and MFCCSA. Each state uses its most recent study year with county prices: 2022 for most states, an earlier year where the NDCP has no 2022 prices (see each state's dataYear). Prices are in that year's dollars and are not adjusted for inflation; the NDCP does not publish inflation-adjusted prices. Annual figures are the weekly price x 52. State medians are DOL's own state-level estimates for the same year (NDCP State-Level Estimates 2022 file, which DOL computes from county prices and county child-population estimates); where DOL has none, we compute a median weighting each county by its households with children under 6 (NDCP H_UNDER6_* variables), which reproduces DOL's figure for 43 of 49 states. The national figure is that weighted median across all counties with 2022 prices. DOL imputed some county prices: it converted hourly, daily or monthly survey prices to weekly, modeled county prices from statewide figures, filled missing age groups, and estimated years with no survey from the price trend between survey years (NDCP technical report, September 2024). Many 2022 county prices are such estimates; per-state counts of modeled and estimated infant center prices are included. Market-rate surveys measure what providers charge, not what families pay after subsidies.",
